<%#
  A field that only applies to one answer, revealed under the answer that needs it.

  Locals: `id` and `shown` are required; `data` and `class` are optional. `shown` is computed on
  the server -- the controller that owns the toggle may reveal, but must never have to un-draw
  (see design.md, "Render the state, do not correct it").

  **The indent and the rule are the whole point.** They say *this belongs to that one option*,
  which nothing else on the page says: a field sitting flush under a three-option radio group
  reads as belonging to all three. GOV.UK, NHS and USWDS all mark a conditional reveal this way
  and it is the only part of the pattern any of them have usability-tested.

  **The numbers are ours, not GOV.UK's.** Theirs -- 18px margin, 4px rule, 33px padding -- are
  sized to a 40px radio. Ours is 16px with a `gap-2` to its label, so: the rule is centred under
  the radio at `ml-1.5` (6px, which is 16/2 - 4/2), and `pl-3.5` (14px) puts the revealed content
  at 24px, exactly the left edge of the label above it. Copying 55px would have indented it to
  nowhere.

  No `aria-expanded` on the trigger, which is where this departs from govuk-frontend. Measured
  with axe on /distributions/new: `aria-controls` alone is clean, and adding `aria-expanded` to
  the three radios raises `aria-allowed-attr` x3 -- ARIA 1.2 does not list it for `role=radio`.
  It *is* allowed on a checkbox (also measured, clean), but using it there and not on the radios
  would make the pattern differ by trigger type for no gain, and on a `<select>` it would be
  actively wrong: on a combobox `aria-expanded` means the listbox is open, not that the
  conditional content is showing. So `aria-controls` everywhere and `aria-expanded` nowhere. The
  behaviour that actually matters is that the revealed field follows its trigger in the DOM, so
  Tab reaches it next.

  The reveal owns 12px above and below (`my-3`), so a single field inside it takes
  `wrapper_html: {class: "mb-0"}` rather than adding its own 16px on top.

  `marked: false` drops the indent and the rule, for content that already carries its own box --
  a callout. Two markings for one relationship is noise, and the callout's tint says "this is a
  distinct block" on its own. It still sits under its trigger, still starts in the right state,
  and still gets audited: `data-conditional-reveal` says which kind it is so the audit does not
  have to infer it.
%>
<%
  marked = local_assigns.fetch(:marked, true)
  reveal_classes = marked ? "my-3 ml-1.5 border-l-4 border-slate-300 pl-3.5" : "my-3"
%>
<div id="<%= id %>"
     class="<%= reveal_classes %> <%= "hidden" unless shown %> <%= local_assigns[:class] %>"
     data-conditional-reveal="<%= marked ? "marked" : "plain" %>"
     <%= tag.attributes(data: local_assigns[:data]) if local_assigns[:data].present? %>>
  <%= yield %>
</div>
